BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (      # The Tale of the River and the Field) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (      In ancient times, the River and the Field existed in separation. The River flowed endlessl) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (        y with water but had nowhere to give its abundance. The Field stretched wide but remaine) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (        d parched and barren.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      One spring, the River made a choice. "I shall send forth my waters," it declared, releasin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (        g its precious flow toward the Field. As the water traveled, the Great Earth multiplied ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (        it threefold, spreading it across the Field's expanse.) Tj ET
